        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Does a Heated Driveway Actually Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The installed price of the snow-melting system itself, meaning the heating cables or tubing, the controls, the sensor and the labor to lay it all out, typically lands somewhere between $12 and $25 per square foot in most US markets. Electric cable and mat systems tend to sit at the lower half of that range for small areas. Hydronic systems, which circulate heated fluid through tubing and need a boiler, tend to land at the top of the range or beyond it once the boiler and mechanical work are included. Prices vary a lot by region, by contractor, and by year, so treat every number on this page as a rough guide rather than a quote.
          </p>

          <p>
            The part people miss is that the heating system is buried inside the driveway. Cables and tubing are embedded in a new concrete slab, set in the base under new asphalt, or laid in the sand bed under pavers. That means you are almost always paying for a new driveway at the same time. A plain concrete driveway already runs roughly $8 to $18 per square foot installed, and asphalt roughly $4 to $10, so a fully heated two-car concrete driveway can easily reach $15,000 to $35,000 all in once demolition, paving and electrical work are added up.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$12-25+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per sq ft, heating system only</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$7k-25k</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical two-car system</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Area saved with tire tracks only</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Tens of $</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Electric cost per big storm</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Size is the biggest driver. A typical two-car driveway is somewhere around 400 to 1,000 square feet depending on length, and the bill scales almost directly with the area you heat. That is why the most common way to bring the price down is not to heat the whole thing. Heating only the two tire tracks, or only the steep section near the street, or only the walkway and the first car length, can cut both the install and the running cost roughly in half.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the short answer is this: budget roughly $12 to $25 per square foot for the system, add the cost of the new surface it goes into, add electrical or boiler work, and then budget a real number per storm to run it. The rest of this guide breaks those pieces down and explains when the math works and when a good snow blower and a bag of concrete-safe ice melt is the better buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Does It Cost by Size and System Type?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table below shows rough installed ranges for the heating system only, not the driveway surface it goes into. We used the $12 to $25 per square foot range and rounded, because the honest truth is that local labor rates, the distance to your electrical panel or boiler room, and the shape of the driveway move the final number more than any precise per-foot price could predict.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Area heated</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. sq ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Electric system (rough)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hydronic system (rough)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Walkway or front steps</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,000-$3,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely worth a boiler alone</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tire tracks, one-car</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150-250</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,500-$5,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,000-$7,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tire tracks, two-car</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300-450</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,500-$9,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,000-$12,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full one-car driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250-400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,000-$8,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5,500-$10,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full two-car driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500-1,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$7,000-$20,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$10,000-$25,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Long or sloped driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$15,000+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$20,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Small areas cost more per square foot because some costs are fixed no matter how much you heat. Every system needs a controller, a snow sensor, a dedicated circuit or a heat source, and a site visit or two from an electrician or plumber. On a 100-square-foot walkway, those fixed costs can be a big share of the bill. On a 900-square-foot driveway, they fade into the per-foot price. That is also why heating a single walkway with hydronic tubing almost never makes sense unless a boiler is already in place and has spare capacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            The surface matters too. Concrete is the most common and most straightforward, because cables or tubing are tied to the reinforcement and the slab is poured over them. Pavers work well because the heating element sits in a sand or mortar bed and the pavers can be lifted for repairs later. Asphalt is possible but more demanding: the cable or tubing must be rated for the heat of hot-mix asphalt, it is usually protected under a layer of base or sand, and rollers have to be used with care so nothing gets crushed. Expect some installers to charge more for asphalt or decline it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that the table excludes the driveway itself. If your existing driveway is in good shape and you only want heat, you are mostly out of luck: the heating has to go in or under the surface, so retrofitting a heated system almost always means tearing out and replacing the slab. A few cable systems are designed to be set into saw-cut grooves in sound existing concrete, but that approach is less common, needs an installer who does it regularly, and is not an option for asphalt or for concrete that is already cracking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electric-vs-hydronic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Should You Choose Electric or Hydronic?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric systems use resistance heating cables or pre-spaced cable mats laid in the driveway and wired to a dedicated circuit, a contactor and a snow-melt controller. They are simpler, faster to install, have no moving parts, and need almost no maintenance. The trade-off is running cost: electricity is usually the most expensive way to make heat, and a driveway snow-melt system draws a lot of it. Electric is usually the better pick for smaller areas, tire tracks, walkways and steps, and for homes without a boiler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydronic systems run a heated mix of water and antifreeze, usually propylene glycol, through PEX tubing embedded in the slab. The heat comes from a boiler, most often natural gas or propane, plus pumps, a manifold, a heat exchanger and controls. Hydronic costs more to install and has parts that need service, but where natural gas is cheap it usually costs less per storm to run than electric. It tends to make the most sense for large driveways, very snowy climates, and new construction where the mechanical room is being planned anyway.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Electric radiant</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hydronic radiant</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Install cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower, roughly $12-$20/sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher, roughly $15-$25+/sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Running cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher per storm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually lower with natural gas</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best for</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small areas, tracks, walkways</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large driveways, heavy snow</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Maintenance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost none</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Boiler service, glycol checks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Repair if damaged</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Locate and splice cable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Locate and repair tubing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Needs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrical capacity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Boiler and mechanical space</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A common hope is to tie a driveway into the house boiler. That is sometimes possible, but snow melting is a big heat load, often larger than the heating needs of the house itself for a full driveway, and it usually happens on the coldest days when the house needs the boiler most. Many installers size a separate boiler for the snow-melt system, or upsize the main one. Get that decision in writing from a heating contractor, because an undersized boiler means a driveway that melts slowly and a house that feels cold during storms.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest rule of thumb: if you are heating under about 300 square feet, or you have no gas service, electric is almost always the simpler and cheaper overall choice. If you are heating a full two-car or longer driveway in a snowy region with natural gas available, hydronic is worth pricing, because over 15 or 20 winters the lower running cost can make up a good part of the higher install price. Either way, ask for both quotes if your contractor offers both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Does It Cost to Run Per Storm?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is where heated driveways surprise people. Residential electric snow-melt systems are commonly designed to deliver somewhere around 30 to 50 watts per square foot, and they need that much power because they are melting snow outdoors against wind and cold air. You can estimate your own cost with simple math: square feet heated, times watts per square foot, divided by 1,000, gives kilowatts. Multiply by your electric rate and by the hours the system runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is a worked example. A 600-square-foot driveway at 40 watts per square foot draws about 24 kilowatts while it runs. At an electric rate of 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, that is roughly $4 per hour. A storm that keeps the system running for eight hours, including warm-up and the time it takes to dry the surface afterward, costs about $33. A long storm or a cold, windy one that keeps it running for a day can cost over $90. Multiply by the number of storms you get each winter and the annual bill can run from a couple of hundred dollars to well over a thousand.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FORMULA</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sq ft x watts per sq ft / 1,000 x hours x your rate</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use the watts per square foot from your installer's design and the rate on your own electric bill. Then sanity check the result against how many storms you actually get. Tire tracks instead of the full slab cut this number about in half.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Hydronic running cost depends on the price of gas or propane and the efficiency of the boiler. With natural gas at typical prices, many homeowners find a hydronic system costs noticeably less per storm than an electric one of the same size, though it is still not cheap, because the heat load is the same; only the fuel is cheaper. Propane often narrows or erases that advantage. Your installer should be able to give you an estimated cost per hour of operation based on the boiler size and your local fuel price. Ask for it in writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Controls make a huge difference. A system that runs only when the sensor detects both freezing temperatures and moisture, and shuts off once the surface is dry, costs far less than one left on manually for the whole day. Some controllers offer an idling mode that keeps the slab just above freezing before a forecast storm so it melts faster when snow starts. Idling improves response but adds hours of run time, so use it only where speed really matters, such as a steep driveway or an accessible entrance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="save" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Can You Lower the Price?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat less area. Tire tracks are the classic compromise: a pair of strips about two feet wide under each car's wheel paths give you a drivable surface without heating the whole slab. You will still have snow between and beside the tracks, but it does not stop the car, and you can walk in the clear track. For a two-car driveway, tracks typically cover roughly 40 to 50 percent of the area, which cuts both the install price and the per-storm bill by about that much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat the parts that matter most. On many properties the real problem is one steep section near the street, the apron where the plow berm lands, or the path from the car to the front door. Heating only those zones, and clearing the flat parts with a shovel or snow blower, often gives most of the benefit for a fraction of the cost. Zoned systems can also be run separately, so the walkway can melt during a light snowfall without running the whole driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Time it with a driveway replacement. Because the surface has to come out anyway, the only extra cost when you are already replacing a cracked driveway is the heating system and its electrical or boiler work. That is by far the cheapest moment to add heat. If your driveway still has years left, it is usually smarter to wait than to tear out sound concrete just to bury cables in it. Our guide to <a href="/concrete-driveway-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete driveway cost</a> shows what the slab alone runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get the controls right. An automatic snow sensor, either a pavement-mounted sensor or an aerial sensor on a post or eave, costs a few hundred dollars more than a manual switch and pays for itself by keeping run time down. Insulation under the heated area, where the installer recommends it, sends more heat up into the snow rather than down into the ground. Ask for the expected watts or BTU per square foot and where it is going, not just a total price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Hidden Costs Should You Expect?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electrical service is the big one for electric systems. A 600-square-foot driveway at 40 watts per square foot is a 24-kilowatt load, which is more than two electric ranges with every burner and the oven on. Many homes with 100-amp or even 200-amp service do not have room for that on top of everything else. Upgrading service or adding a subpanel can add a few thousand dollars, and the utility may need to be involved. Get an electrician to look at your panel before you get attached to a heated driveway quote.
          </p>

          <p>
            Demolition and disposal of the old driveway are often quoted separately. Removing an old concrete slab and hauling it away can add a few dollars per square foot. If the base underneath is poor, or drainage has to be fixed, that is more. Hydronic systems add boiler installation, venting, gas piping, a mechanical location and sometimes a permit for the gas work. Electric systems need permits too in most places, plus inspections before the slab is poured.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meltwater has to go somewhere. A heated driveway turns snow into water, and that water runs to the low edge, where it can refreeze into an ice ridge if the edge is not heated or drained. Good installs include a drain or a heated strip at the bottom, or are designed so water runs onto ground that can absorb it. If yours sheds onto a sidewalk, you may be creating a new ice problem on someone else's walk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there are repair and long-term costs. Embedded cables and tubing are durable, and electric systems often carry long manufacturer warranties, but a cable cut by a saw, a fence post or a utility crew is expensive to find and fix. Some systems include a fault indicator or are tested at install so problems show up early. Hydronic systems need periodic boiler service and glycol checks. And if the heat ever fails completely, you have an expensive driveway that you shovel like everyone else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is a Heated Driveway Worth It Compared to Ice Melt and a Snow Blower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most households, honestly, no. A good two-stage snow blower typically costs somewhere around $800 to $2,000 and lasts a decade or more with basic care. A season of concrete-safe ice melt for the walk and steps is usually well under $100. A plow service, where available, often costs a few hundred dollars a season. Against a $10,000 to $35,000 heated driveway plus a running bill, the payback in pure dollars is measured in decades, and for many homes it never arrives.
          </p>

          <p>
            It can be worth it for specific situations. A steep driveway that becomes impassable with an inch of ice, a household member with a disability or heart condition who should not be shoveling, a long driveway in heavy snow country, or a medical professional who has to leave at 4 a.m. regardless of weather are all real reasons. So is avoiding de-icers entirely to protect new or decorative concrete, though applying the right product correctly gets you most of that benefit for far less; see our <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best ice melt for concrete</a> guide for the safest options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resale value is a weak argument. A heated driveway is a nice feature in high-end snowy markets, but buyers rarely pay back the full install cost, and some see the running cost as a liability. Treat it as a quality-of-life purchase, not an investment. If you will not be in the house for at least several winters, the numbers are even harder to justify.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you decide against heat, put the money into a solid winter routine instead. Seal cracks before the freeze, use a snow blower or a good shovel early in the storm, and use a de-icer that will not scale your slab. Our <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-driveway-for-winter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">guide to preparing a driveway for winter</a> covers the steps. And if salt damage is your worry, read our explainer on <a href="/does-salt-damage-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether salt damages concrete</a> before assuming you need a heated slab to avoid it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pricing the heat but not the driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The heating system is buried in or under the surface, so almost every project includes a new slab, asphalt or paver install. Comparing a $9,000 heating quote against a snow blower is misleading if the real total, with demolition and new concrete, is closer to $20,000. Always ask for the complete number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the electrical panel check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An electric system on a full two-car driveway can draw over 20 kilowatts. Many homes need a service upgrade or a new subpanel to carry that, which can add thousands. Have an electrician confirm your capacity before signing anything, or the cheapest quote can become the most expensive one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it manually</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Turning the system on when it starts snowing and forgetting it for a day can double or triple the cost of a storm. An automatic sensor that starts the system when it is cold and wet and stops it when the slab is dry is worth the extra few hundred dollars on any system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring where the meltwater goes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Melted snow runs downhill and refreezes where the heat stops. Without a drain, a heated edge strip or a planned runoff path, you can trade a snowy driveway for an ice ridge at the street or a frozen sidewalk. Ask the installer exactly where the water goes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heating the whole slab by default</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many homeowners only need the tire tracks, a steep section or the walkway. Heating everything doubles the install and the running cost for snow that would not have stopped the car. Price a tracks-only or zoned layout before committing to full coverage, and compare the per-storm running cost of each option too.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you add heat to an existing driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not without replacing it. Heating cables and tubing are embedded in the slab or set in the base below the surface, so most retrofits mean tearing out and repouring. A few electric cable systems can be set into saw-cut grooves in sound existing concrete, but that is a specialized install and not an option for asphalt or cracked slabs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a heated driveway last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The embedded cable or tubing is designed to last as long as the slab around it, often decades, and electric cables commonly carry long manufacturer warranties. Controls, sensors, pumps and boilers wear out sooner and get replaced on their own schedules. Physical damage from cutting, drilling or a failing slab is the most common way an embedded system dies early.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do heated driveways melt snow instantly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A system starting from a cold slab can take a while to begin melting, and heavy snowfall can outpace it. That is why some controllers offer an idling mode that keeps the slab just above freezing before a forecast storm. Idling melts faster once snow starts but adds run time and cost, so most homes use it only where speed matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is electric or hydronic cheaper to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hydronic is usually cheaper per storm where natural gas is available at typical prices, because gas heat costs less than electric resistance heat. With propane or expensive gas, the gap narrows or disappears. Electric is cheaper to install and maintain, so for small areas it often wins on total cost even if each storm costs more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do heated driveways work in extreme cold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They work, but they work harder. In very cold, windy weather a system loses more heat to the air and melts snow more slowly, and run time goes up. Systems in the coldest regions are designed for higher output per square foot. Ask your installer what conditions the design is sized for and how it performs below about 10 degrees.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a heated driveway need a permit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most places, yes. Electric systems need electrical permits and inspections, usually before the slab is poured so the inspector can see the cable. Hydronic systems need permits for the boiler, gas piping and venting. A reputable installer pulls the permits and schedules the inspections; be wary of any quote that suggests skipping them to save time or money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you still use ice melt on a heated driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You should not need it on the heated areas, and avoiding de-icers is one of the benefits. On unheated walkways, steps and edges, use a concrete-safe product sparingly. Avoid piling salty snow from the street or other areas onto the heated slab, since the meltwater still carries chloride into the concrete and can scale the surface over time.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

