        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A heated driveway typically costs roughly $12 to $25 or more per square foot for the heating system, plus the new surface it is buried in, plus electrical or boiler work, plus a real running cost every time it snows. Electric is cheaper to install and suits small areas and tire tracks; hydronic costs more up front but usually less to run on natural gas for large driveways.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most homes, a good snow blower and a concrete-safe ice melt deliver a clear driveway for a small fraction of the price. A heated driveway earns its cost when the slope is steep, someone cannot safely shovel, or you are replacing the driveway anyway and can add heat to just the tracks or the trouble spots. If you go ahead, insist on an automatic sensor, a plan for the meltwater and a complete written quote.
          </p>
        </div>

